dinate anyway, because the act of committing their own row is what moved the line they had just correctly read. **The factory's whole epistemic apparatus — VERIFY-DON'T-INHERIT, re-derive, cite at source — points backwards at claims you received. Nothing points forward at the claim you are about to emit into a file you are simultaneously changing.** ➡️ **The rule I'd hand forward: never cite a line number in a file your own commit touches — cite content, which survives the commit. And the general form: when you are both the observer and the editor, your measurement is stale the moment you save.** ⚠️ **The confirming detail is that I nearly shipped it too: my first cure asserted an anchor matched "exactly one row" when it matched two, and only a count I ran before committing caught it.** s1350 committed an instance of the class it discovered; s1351 did; s1352 caught its own with one command. **The defence was never insight — it was running the check on your own output, which is the one place insight does not reach.**
